313: 		break;
314: 
315: 	default:	/*  #573: CSR3 is BSWP|ACON|BCON; bits 15:3 "Reserved. Read as zeroes."  */
316: 		d->reg[r] = x & 0x0007;
317: 	}
318: ```
319: 
320: `r` is `d->reg_select`, which the RAP arm stores as `idata & (N_REGISTERS - 1)` (S1), so `default:` after explicit
321: `case 1:` and `case 2:` is exactly CSR3.  **Alternative form (B):** one table,
322: `static const uint16_t le_csr_wmask[N_REGISTERS] = { 0xffff, 0xfffe, 0x00ff, 0x0007 };` and
323: `d->reg[r] = x & le_csr_wmask[r];` in the existing `default:` arm -- a one-line diff whose index-0 entry is never used
324: (CSR0 is `case 0:`), which is the argument against it.  Both are "port rpajarola e108b54" in substance; the research row
325: records his masks, not his form.
326: 
327: **3.2 The consumer that changes.**  `le_chip_init()` (8.1, line 179) builds `init_block_addr = (d->reg[1] & 0xffff) +
328: ((d->reg[2] & 0xff) << 16)` and at 180-182 warns `"WARNING! address not word aligned?"` when it is odd.  With CSR1's
329: bit 0 dropped at the store, that condition can never be true: **the check becomes unreachable code**.  Options:
330: (i) delete the three lines, recording the datasheet reason at the store; (ii) leave it (a check that cannot fire --
331: this project's own "check-that-cannot-fail" class, i.e. a wrong record in product code); (iii) move the diagnostic to
332: the CSR1 store, latched `(once)`, firing when a guest writes bit 0 set (the datasheet's "Must be zero" being violated).
333: **Proposal: (i)**, in the same change (R14: L4-adjacent -- product code asserting a check the fix makes impossible).
334: The `(d->reg[2] & 0xff)` in the same expression becomes redundant but harmless; left alone.
335: 
336: **3.3 Who else runs this model.**  `dev_le_init` is called from `machine_pmax.c` for the KN01 (3100, line 129) and the
337: KN230 (line 708), from `dev_turbochannel.c:227` (the PMAD-AA option on the 5000/200), and from `dev_luna88k.c:1086`
338: (a big-endian host; `is_bigendian` affects SRAM byte order only, 8.1).  The register path is shared, so the fix changes
339: CSR1-3 read-back on all of them.  The witness machine (3100) is the one every DECstation gate boots.
340: 
341: **3.4 Sources, and where they differ (D1-D4).**
342: 
343: * **D1 -- CSR1 bit 0.**  The Am79C90 datasheet says bit 0 *"Must be zero."* -- a constraint on what the host WRITES --
344:   and its register figure draws the bit as `'0'`; it does NOT say "read as zeroes" for it, as it does for CSR2 15:8 and
345:   CSR3 15:3.  rpajarola masks it (`&= 0xfffe`).  The weakest of the three premises (P1).
346: * **D2 -- which chip.**  The DECstation 3100 carries the Am7990 (LANCE); the two datasheets on disk are the Am79C90
347:   (C-LANCE, "PRELIMINARY").  The Am7990 Technical Manual (1986) draws the same layout (CSR1 `IADR` with a `0` at bit 0;
348:   CSR2 `RES` / `IADR` split at 8; CSR3 `RES` / `BSWP ACON BCON`) and says *"CSR3 is cleared"* by reset and *"CSR1, CSR2
349:   undefined by reset"*, but its extracted text carries no "read as zeroes" sentence (P2).
350: * **D3 -- the drivers never set the bits.**  NetBSD `lance.c` writes `LE_CSR1, a` / `LE_CSR2, a >> 16` / `LE_CSR3,
351:   sc->sc_conf3`; Linux `declance.c` writes `leptr & 0xFFFF` / `leptr >> 16` / `lp->busmaster_regval` (9.3).  With a
352:   24-bit DMA address `a >> 16` has bits 15:8 clear, so a shipped driver never observes the defect; a guest sees it only
353:   by reading back after writing unimplemented bits (a diagnostic, a PROM self-test, a probe).  The severity MED stands on
354:   that reading (P10).
355: * **D4 -- what the datasheet says that the model does not do, and this round does NOT touch:** CSR1-3 are *"Accessible
356:   only when the STOP bit of CSR0 is a ONE"* (the model accepts them always -- the comment at 996-1001 says Ultrix writes
357:   them with STOP clear); *"CSR3 is cleared by RESET or by setting the STOP bit in CSR0"* (the model's `le_register_fix`
358:   clears only IDON on STOP, "Works with Ultrix", 802-812); *"CSR1, CSR2 undefined by reset"* (the model zeroes them).
359:   Filing candidates (Q3e), not rows.
360: 
361: **3.5 The option set** -- for a WRONG-RESULT register decode, not the de-fatalisation set (R11):
362: **M1 mask at the store** (3.1; rpajarola's form; the stored value IS the chip's value, so every reader of `reg[]` sees
363: it) A� **M2 mask at the read-back** (`DEVICE_ACCESS(le)` `case 0:` read, 8.3: `odata = d->reg[sel] & mask[sel]`; the
364: stored value keeps the written bits and the INIT consumer keeps its parity check live) A� **M3 both** A�
365: **M4 decline-and-announce a write that sets reserved bits** (the datasheet's "Write as zeroes" violated -- an addition
366: needing a warrant under R10; no shipped driver does it, so none is offered) A� **M5 document only**.  **Proposal: M1.**
367: Grep the fork for the existing idiom before choosing (Q1d): the nearest precedent I know is `#572`'s select-byte
368: read-back (`dev_wdc.c`, `sdh_resv = idata & 0xa0`), which stores the implemented bits at the write.
369: 
